  <!-- § 09 misread -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">09</span><h2>How to misread this number</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li>
        <span class="no">i.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"Industry grew 7%, so the economy grew 7%."</h3>
          <p>Industry is under a third of GDP, and this index covers only its organised, goods-producing slice. Services, the larger half of the economy, are elsewhere entirely. The IIP is a pulse, not the patient.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">ii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"One strong month is a boom."</h3>
          <p>This is the most volatile major series in Indian statistics. A festival calendar shift can move a print by two points; a single large factory's shutdown can move an industry. Three-month averages are the minimum unit of belief.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"The first print is the number."</h3>
          <p>Quick estimates are revised as late returns arrive, and the revisions are not noise around zero: they cluster. Citing the first print of two years ago against the final print of last year mixes vintages.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iv.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"Growth recovered, so output recovered."</h3>
          <p>Every growth rate is a ratio to a year ago. In April 2021 the index printed +134%: and output was still below its pre-pandemic level. The spike measured the hole in the denominator, not the height of the economy.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">v.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"The two bases tell different stories, so one is wrong."</h3>
          <p>The 2011–12 and 2022–23 series use different items, different factories, different weights. Where they overlap they routinely disagree by a point. That is what changing the ruler does. Compare within a base, never across the seam.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">vi.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"A young index can be read like an old one."</h3>
          <p>A new index, the ISP is one, has no seasonal history: nobody knows what its normal festival month or year-end looks like, because none has happened twice. Until the same month has recurred for years, it supports statements about levels, not about momentum. Where the seasonal shape is unknown, the honest reading is "we do not know".</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

